{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime shift bias: when the recent history is in the high-60s/low-70s and momentum turns down, the model stays anchored too high. Apply a downward level shift of ~4–8 €/t across the 2–4 week horizon once a drawdown starts (i.e., after the local peak).",
    "Horizon-dependent error: near-term (days 1–7) forecasts are slightly low during the final upswing (+0–1.5 €/t), but medium/long horizon (days 10–30) becomes strongly too high (+5–12 €/t). Use a correction that increases with horizon after a peak: subtract ~0–2 €/t (d1–7), ~3–6 €/t (d8–15), ~6–12 €/t (d16–30).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following a price spike/peak (e.g., >71–73), the model extrapolates elevated levels and misses subsequent sell-offs. If the last observed price is >2 €/t above the 10–20d average, cap the forecast path and force faster mean reversion (pull 50–70% back toward the 20d mean by day 15).",
    "Underestimation of mean reversion speed: true outcomes revert downward much faster than the model. Increase reversion speed whenever 5–10d momentum turns negative (e.g., 5d MA < 10d MA): blend forecasts toward the 20d mean with a weight that grows with horizon (e.g., 20% at d5 → 60% at d20).",
    "Trend-lag: the model reacts late to turning points (keeps rising/flat while realized starts falling). Use a turning-point trigger: if the last 3–5 realized closes are down and volatility is elevated, shift the entire forecast slope downward (impose a negative drift of ~0.2–0.4 €/t/day for the next 10–15 days)."
  ]
}
